  "query_preview": "..\n\nLake aggregate stratus and light shower activity associated with the \ndeparting low continue to impact the northern Saginaw Valley and \nThumb this morning. Will see this moisture vacate SE Michigan \nthrough the morning as high pressure drifts overhead and winds back \nto the southwest. The backing winds initiate a period of warm \nadvection this afternoon that boosts h8 temperatures by roughly +5 \nC, although slightly displaced from peak heating so highs still only\nreach the mid 50s. Another low amplitude wave and weak front quickly\nmove through tonight, although the surface trough is shallow with\nminimal impact on the thermal gradient. Tuesday is another dry day, \nwith an even deeper layer of static stability than today. This \naffords plenty of sunshine and full realization of the warm \nadvection to bring highs into the low 60s. \n\nThe back half of the work week becomes more unsettled, driven on the \nlarge scale by amplification of the geopotential height field. \nLongwave troughing becomes established across eastern CONUS, with \nthe bulk of height falls occurring Wednesday as a Pacific wave \nejects out of the Intermountain West. The ensemble envelope is still \nclustered into a few different solutions, depending on if the \nsurface low takes a direct path toward SE Michigan or dives south \nacross the Ohio Valley. Both solutions generate rain opportunities \nfor SE Michigan, although the latter is associated with elevated \nfrontal forcing and would likely result in a narrower area of \nprecipitation. NBM chance PoPs are reflective of the uncertainty, \nincluding some of the dry solutions as well where the low tracks \neven further south across the Tennessee Valley. \n\nHigher confidence item with this setup is the strong cold advection \nthat follows Thursday into the weekend. Highs during this timeframe \nwill be in the 40s and lows in the upper 20s to low 30s. Coldest \nmorning looks to be Saturday, corresponding to the core of the \nthermal trough settling overhead. The strength of cold advection \nwill also activate lake moisture flux behind the low, with northerly \nflow showing a convergent signal across the major axis of Lake Huron \nespecially Friday. Even with above freezing temperatures, dewpoints \nhovering near 32 F and wet bulb zero heights between 1.0-2.0 kft agl \nmean snowflakes cannot be ruled out especially if higher intensity \nrates develop. \n\nOne of the key features of this time period will be a deep upper low \nthat anchors over Quebec, causing any inbound shortwaves to shear \nout and fragment as they encounter the sharp deformation zone on the \nwestern flank of the low. This causes each impulse to deflect south \ninto the Upper Midwest/Great Lakes region, where any phased or well-\ntimed impulse could provide enough synoptic ascent to enhance the \nlake effect. This equates to an unsettled precipitation forecast \nThursday into the weekend even for interior portions of Lower \nMichigan.",
